WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our next guest is an
award-winning writer whose wor

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know from
"Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:06.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
the TBS series "Miracle
Workers," "An American Pickle.

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:10.076
His latest book, "New Teeth,
is available on July 27th.

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.946
Please welcome back to the sho
our very good friend Simon Rich.

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:14.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hello, Simon.
It's so good to see you.

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:17.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Great to see you.
Thanks for having me.

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:17.951 line:92%
-I'm thrilled to have you.

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:22.822
I'm wondering how, as a writer
you managed during the pandemic.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:25.625
I've always known you to b
an incredibly prolific --

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:28.862
like, somebody who works
very hard at their craft.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:31.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm guessing -- and I hope thi
doesn't come off wrong --

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.500 line:92%
that you might have thrived.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.903 size:84% position:42%
-Yeah. I tried to be as
productive as possible

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:39.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
and putting as many hours
as ever, if not more,

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:43.543
but seeing you hold up the book,
I now am a little self-conscious

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:45.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause I feel like the boo
should be longer,

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:49.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
given the severity and lengt
of the global pandemic.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:54.054
Like, if I can't crack 300 pages
in 2020,

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:55.989
then, you know, it's never
going to happen, probably.

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:56.723 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-Yeah, I'm looking now.

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:59.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
Acknowledgements is starting
at like 225.

00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:02.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
So that's pretty weak.
-Exactly.

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:04.531 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-You have two daughters.

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:07.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
What are they,
four and not even a year?

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.935 line:92%
-Yeah, four and six months

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:11.371 line:92%
Yeah, it's pretty amazing.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:15.175 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's very difficult,
as you know, but the thing

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:18.078
that's great about kids is
it takes them a pretty long time

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:19.446 line:92%
to be able to form memories.

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:22.916
So, as a parent, like, the first
four years of parenting,

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:25.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's basically
like exhibition baseball.

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
It doesn't really count.

00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:29.456 line:92%
There's no true record of it

00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:30.490 size:81% position:41% line:92%
It's still low stakes.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like Tim Tebow is still ou
there taking a pass.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.628 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It must be, you know,
nice to have, like,

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:41.935
a life-changing experience
like children, when you ar

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:44.104
someone who's looking for
new things to write about.

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:47.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously, it's a wealth
of subject matter.

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:50.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. I mean, material
isn't like the sole reason

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:53.646 align:start size:94% position:6%
why I had kids, but it's,
like, I would say,

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
probably somewhere
in the top 10.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:57.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You've had a lot of
stories published

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:58.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
over the years
in "The New Yorker."

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:01.821 align:start size:94% position:6%
One of the stories is in
this collection, "The Big Nap.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:06.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
It must still be nice to b
published in "The New Yorker."

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:07.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm wondering,
over the years,

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
have you ever gotten a sense
you ever been on a subway

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:11.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
or anything and realized
people are reading

00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:14.834 size:84% position:42%
either a story of yours
or one of your books?

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. It's happened probably
like, you know,

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:20.006 line:92%
three or four times in my life

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
Always on the subway
in New York City.

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:23.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's just
absolutely terrifying.

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:26.179
And I just lock eyes with them
creepily from across the train

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:30.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm just, like, so anxious
about whether or not

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:32.018 size:88% position:44%
they're going to smirk,
which is really the best

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:34.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
you could ever hope for
from a piece of written comedy

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:37.724
And there is nothing more
horrible than them reading

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:42.095 align:start size:94% position:6%
the first paragraph and then
just turning the page.

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:45.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I guess if you're like
an actor or a stand-up,

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:47.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's your day-to-day
existence.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I've always been so shocke
at the bravery of somebody

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:53.072
who can kind of live in that
terror for an hour at a time

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:57.710
'Cause I can barely handle i
for a few fleeting seconds

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.379 line:92%
-We worked together at "SNL.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:02.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was a time I look back on
fondly, to the point that

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:06.719
I probably overlook exactl
how much stress and anxiet

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.021 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
filled those years.

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:12.125
You've done a lot since you've
left "SNL," created two shows,

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:15.828
wrote for "The Simpsons,"
multiple books, films.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.430 line:92%
Is it true that that's still

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.699 align:start size:94% position:6%
where all your stress dreams
come from?

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:25.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. I have exclusively
"SNL" nightmares.

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:28.441
I have only had
"Saturday Night Live" nightmares

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:30.643 size:84% position:42% line:92%
since leaving the show.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's always pretty much
the same.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:35.048 align:start size:94% position:6%
Lorne's on a horse.
-Uh-huh.

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:35.782 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.782 --> 00:03:39.052
-He's looking down at me
from a very great height.

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:42.855 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there's always been
some kind of paperwork snafu

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:48.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
and as a result, I have to g
on camera and perform,

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:51.564 line:92%
which is not my job as a writer.

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:55.268
But he's always saying, "You
have to go out there right now."

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:58.371 line:92%
Then he speeds off on his horse.

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:02.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'll say in these dreams
you are in all of them.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:03.810 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay.
-And you were my --

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
you were my head writer
when I worked for that show.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:08.014 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you were also
a real mentor figure.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:11.985
And you were really nice to me
during that time in my life.

00:04:11.985 --> 00:04:14.520 size:75% position:38%
But in these dreams,
you do not help me.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.622 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:19.392
You are not openly antagonistic,
but you never try --

00:04:19.392 --> 00:04:20.727 line:92%
you never listen to reason

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:22.895 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
You're always like,

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:25.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Lorne's already galloped
to the control room."

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:25.932 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:29.269
-"And I hope you know the lyrics
to 'Poker Face,'

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:31.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause Lady Gaga's contrac
didn't go through,

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:33.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you have to do it
right now."

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're never pitching like
"Oh, I could ask Maya Rudolph.

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.845 align:start size:94% position:6%
She's a great singer, and she'
already in the building."

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:40.446 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're just like,
"It has to be you."

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Once the horse leaves,
that's it.

00:04:43.016 --> 00:04:45.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
We got to go.
-I don't know why it's a horse

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:47.487 line:92%
I think it's a Canadian thing.

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:48.488 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:50.456 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But have you ever had
that same dream?

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:52.225 line:92%
Because, if so, you know -

00:04:52.225 --> 00:04:54.427 line:92%
-All my dreams are entirely --

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:59.365
My "SNL" dreams are always tha
Wally is nowhere to be seen.

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:00.867 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that the cue cards
are nowhere there.

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:05.238
And it's always someone saying
like, "But you know it, right?

00:05:05.238 --> 00:05:09.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean like, "Oy, oy, oy.
I do not."

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:11.210 align:start size:94% position:6%
You also went from "SNL"
to Pixar, which,

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you think about,
like, the writing process

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:16.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the most jarring transition
you can imagine.

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:18.017 line:92%
-Yeah. It was like weeks later

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:21.054 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was completely strange,
because at "SNL,"

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:25.625
you have six days to creat
90 minutes of entertainment.

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:27.393 line:92%
And then, at Pixar it's like

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.462 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have six years
to create 90 minutes.

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:32.865
And so, leaving an environment
where literally Friday night

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:36.035
it's like, "Well, we have
no cold open or monologue.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:36.703 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Like, we need to --"

00:05:36.703 --> 00:05:40.106 size:88% position:44%
And people are in tears,
racing around, frantic.

00:05:40.106 --> 00:05:42.608 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then, like, three week
later I'm at Pixar,

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:44.844 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's like --
the meetings are like,

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:47.046
"Alright, we've been at this
three years, we really need to

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:52.218
like, pick a lane in terms o
this character's eyebrows now.

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:55.054
I think we need to --
we don't need to crack it today,

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:57.957 align:start size:94% position:6%
but let's start to dial in
these eyebrows."

00:05:57.957 --> 00:06:03.029
And people are just in the poo
and lazing about in hammocks

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:07.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I guess I didn't know
you were actual classmates

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:07.900 size:88% position:44% line:92%
But you went to Harvard,

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you were classmates with
Mark Zuckerberg.

00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:13.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
Did you ever cross paths wit
him then?

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:18.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. So, I never met him
at Harvard.

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:21.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
We were in the same class,
but I never met him.

00:06:21.114 --> 00:06:23.583 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's 'cause we hung ou
with different crowds.

00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:26.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was more in
the writing comedy crowd,

00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:31.224
and he was more focused on
developing a vise-like gri

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:33.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
on democracy
and dominating the world.

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:35.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think he hung out wit
different folks.

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:40.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I do remember
when Facebook started.

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:42.802 line:92%
I remember that week and -

00:06:42.802 --> 00:06:44.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
or The Facebook
as it was known then.

00:06:44.804 --> 00:06:49.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I learned about it
from what everybody considered

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:51.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be the creepiest guy
at Harvard,

00:06:51.878 --> 00:06:54.547
which is really -- that's --
"creepiest guy at Harvard" i

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:56.682 align:start size:94% position:6%
like saying like fastest skate
in the NHL.

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:59.685
It's like a big -- you know,
it's a big deal, and he --

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:00.420 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-A lot of competition.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
-A lot of competition for that
yeah, for that spot.

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:06.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he called everybody over
and he was like,

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, everybody, check out
this new website

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:11.931 line:92%
that lets you stalk girls.

00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:13.499 line:92%
And he showed us The Facebook.

00:07:13.499 --> 00:07:15.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was like, "You just typ
in the girl's name,

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:19.505
there's their face, there'
the list of books she likes,

00:07:19.505 --> 00:07:21.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
so you can pretend
that you like them too."

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:24.811
And then, back then, durin
the early days of Facebook

00:07:24.811 --> 00:07:27.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
it would often list
their class schedule.

00:07:27.146 --> 00:07:31.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you could physically trac
somebody in real time.

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:33.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I remember this guy
literally,

00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:34.120 line:92%
he was like, "Science Center B."

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.989
And he shut his computer,
and he grabbed whatever

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:39.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
Dave Eggers novel
she had said she liked

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:40.359 line:92%
and just ran out of the room

00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:41.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I looked at everybody
and I said,

00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:43.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Okay, I am making a predictio
right now.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:48.734 align:start size:94% position:6%
This Mark kid, he is going t
get in a lot of trouble."

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:49.502 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:52.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
"He's going to get a dean'
warning at least."

00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:55.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
And everybody kind of nodded
solemnly.

00:07:55.575 --> 00:07:57.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I was wrong.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.313 line:92%
He became very, very successful.

00:08:00.313 --> 00:08:01.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
But that's why
I've never been on Facebook,

00:08:01.881 --> 00:08:05.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I associate it with,
like, truly this, like,

00:08:05.785 --> 00:08:09.489 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
creepy, evil dude,

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:12.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I just think Facebook
is really awful,

00:08:12.825 --> 00:08:14.794 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
but I do invest.

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:15.394 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:08:15.394 --> 00:08:17.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I mean, you have to
-Yeah.

00:08:17.296 --> 00:08:20.766
'Cause Zuckerberg isn't the only
Jew from the class of '06.

00:08:20.766 --> 00:08:24.637 size:88% position:44%
I have two bat mitzvahs
to pay for, so you know.

00:08:24.637 --> 00:08:29.208 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Sure. Nobody would judge yo
for that, Simon.

00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:30.576 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah, exactly.

00:08:30.576 --> 00:08:32.845 size:81% position:41%
-Buddy, it is so good
to see you, as always.

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.213 line:92%
Congratulations on the book.

00:08:34.213 --> 00:08:36.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
"New Teeth" is available
everywhere July 27th.

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:37.683 line:92%
Please remember to support

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:39.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
your local and independent
book stores.

